desc:
During its first phase, the internet seemed to develop as a free space without regulation or interference of public or private authorities. Gradually, however, the internet is becoming increasingly regulated. International organizations, private companies, and states try to shape it according to their preferences. This course will look at the evolution of global internet governance. It focuses on the changing role of key actors and conflicts between them – the conflict between private or public oversight, the rise of multistakeholderism, and the attempts of democratic as well as authoritarian states to increase their regulatory grip. It also focuses on key issues of global internet governance such as privacy protection, content control, and cyber security. The course starts from the assumption that despite its dynamics, internet governance is politics like any other and can be understood with standard social science tools, and with concepts from international relations and political science in particular.

Course Goals:
After completing the course:
• you are able to distinguish between and evaluate the roles of different actors in internet governance;
• you are able to discuss the causes and consequences of conflicts between different actors in internet governance;
• you are able to critically reflect upon arguments and assumptions that authors in internet governance make;
• you are able to give input for a session on one of the topics of internet governance;
• you are able to ask and discuss a scientifically and socially relevant research question on one of the topics of internet governance.

Course Contents:
The course starts with a session introducing you to the topic of internet governance. In the second, third, and sixth class, we will discuss the role of specific actors in and the institutional landscape of internet governance. For instance, we will discuss what role the state plays in internet governance and assess if its importance is declining with the transnational character of the internet. The fourth and fifth sessions will be in the form of an assignment. The seventh until the thirteenth session will be led by students’ presentations and critical input and will cover topics such as content control, data governance, and platform governance. In the fourteenth session, we will conclude and reflect upon the course.
